  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ for all compatible matrices $A$ and $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A+B)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A+B)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ is the row space of $({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})^{T}=A+B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A+B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A+B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $A+B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A+B,$ we actually multiply each row of $A+B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A+B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(A+B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14198391/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
